### Why must consumers be idempotent?

At-least-once delivery means a record can be redelivered. Without idempotent processing, a redelivery causes double side effects.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class IdempotentProcessing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String tradeId = "TRD-001";
        BigDecimal amount = new BigDecimal("50000.00");

        // WRONG: non-idempotent -- double-settles on redelivery
        System.out.println("WRONG: settle(tradeId, amount) -- no dedup check");
        System.out.println("  First delivery  : settle 50000 INR");
        System.out.println("  Redelivery      : settle 50000 INR AGAIN!");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: idempotent -- keyed on trade id
        System.out.println("CORRECT: if (!settled(tradeId)) settle(tradeId, amount)");
        System.out.println("  First delivery  : settle 50000 INR");
        System.out.println("  Redelivery      : already settled, SKIP");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: settle(tradeId, amount) -- no dedup check
  First delivery  : settle 50000 INR
  Redelivery      : settle 50000 INR AGAIN!

CORRECT: if (!settled(tradeId)) settle(tradeId, amount)
  First delivery  : settle 50000 INR
  Redelivery      : already settled, SKIP
```

### How does the dead letter queue fit?

Guaranteed delivery does not mean guaranteed success for a broken record. It means the record is never silently lost. A DLQ isolates unprocessable records so the partition keeps flowing.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class GuaranteedDeliveryMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: acks=1 with min.insync.replicas=2");
        System.out.println("  acks=1 never checks ISR -- min.isr is useless");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: auto.commit=true for guaranteed delivery");
        System.out.println("  Offset advances before processing finishes");
        System.out.println("  Crash = data loss");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: no DLQ -- poison pill blocks entire partition");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: acks=1 with min.insync.replicas=2
  acks=1 never checks ISR -- min.isr is useless

WRONG: auto.commit=true for guaranteed delivery
  Offset advances before processing finishes
  Crash = data loss

WRONG: no DLQ -- poison pill blocks entire partition
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Guaranteed delivery is end to end. A weak link anywhere breaks it. At-least-once delivery with idempotent processing is the practical shape of exactly-once for most banking systems. Full transactions are reserved for places that truly need atomic multi-write. Everything must be monitored: ISR, producer errors, consumer lag, DLT depth.
